---
/**
 * Camden, 14 November 2026. A draft for Kaspar to read on the review link
 * (F2F ops clients/structure-and-style/areas-review); held noindex by
 * areaCamdenCheck until he has read it and his changes are in.
 *
 * Structure and sources: ops repo research/area-pages-2026-11-14/README.md
 * (Camden), with every local fact from facts-camden-2026-11-14.md, all read
 * 14 Nov 2026: Camden's Camden Town Conservation Area appraisal (2007, S1),
 * its Camden Square appraisal (2011, S2), the Alan Baxter heritage statement
 * for a Jeffreys Street house on Camden's planning file, which quotes list
 * entry 1379153 (S5), and Camden's listed buildings, parking zone and trade
 * permit pages (S9, S11, S13). Scope is Camden Town and the streets around
 * it, not the borough.
 *
 * FACT LIST (area page rules, section 4 item 4):
 *   - Based in Camden Town: home page, Denis 2 Sep 2026, at most once a page.
 *   - Many early and mid 19th century terraces, many listed: S1.
 *   - Albert Street, Arlington Road and Mornington Terrace on a planned grid,
 *     1820 to 1850; yellow stock brick, typically three storeys over a
 *     basement, mostly painted timber sashes, cast-iron railings: S1.
 *   - Mornington Terrace (1840s, taller and grander, continuous cast-iron
 *     balconies); Albert Street (George Bassett, 1844 to 1848, many still
 *     single family homes; Nos 22 to 46 the only original mansard attic;
 *     front gardens up to 5 metres south of Delancey Street; Tudor Lodge,
 *     1843, Grade II, reputedly the earliest surviving purpose-built
 *     artist's studio): S1.
 *   - Mornington Crescent (three curved terraces of four storeys over
 *     basements, mansard roofs and dormers; railway cuttings behind, the old
 *     Carreras factory in front): S1.
 *   - Arlington Road west side (1820s to about 1840; a roof form the
 *     appraisal calls very distinct, with large rear chimney stacks and long
 *     stair windows): S1.
 *   - Jeffreys Street's north terrace (about 1816 to 1825, Grade II): S5,
 *     quoting the list entry. The staircase, plaster cornice, doors and
 *     skirting of one house there: S5 (one house, so the page says one).
 *   - Camden Square's steep steps and semi-basements: S2.
 *   - The interior of a listed house is protected; significant work inside
 *     can need consent: S9, S14. The listed building map: S9.
 *   - Trade permits (tradespeople working for a resident; resident bays and
 *     certain yellow lines; no space guaranteed): S13. The street search on
 *     the parking zones page: S11.
 *
 * WHOSE WORDS.
 *   - Already approved and live elsewhere: "We are a joinery company based
 *     in Camden Town" (home page). Lorenzo I's review, whole.
 *   - His own facts, reworded only to fit: q31 (walls rarely straight); the
 *     built-in wardrobe guide he approved (24 Oct 2026): "Note what ends up
 *     on a chair or in another room", the drawer section that needs a door
 *     opening giving access to its full width, and its loft example of
 *     "wardrobes arranged symmetrically around an oak window seat"; his
 *     Hampstead line on finishing "neatly below original cornices" (10 Nov
 *     2026); stage04 (sections ready for assembly); conduct fact 6 (stair
 *     protection); q35 and q36; the price card lines as approved on Hampstead.
 *   - New copy, ours, for him to check: everything else.
 *
 * Street names are Camden's documents', describing the houses. None is a
 * place we claim to have worked.
 *
 * PRODUCT-LED, 14 Nov 2026 (a founder: commercial intent, "We don't need
 * carpenter and get a lead that is looking for someone to install a door").
 * The address moved to /areas/camden/; the title, heading, kicker and
 * description lead with fitted and bespoke wardrobes (areas.ts).
 *
 * ON THE PRIMROSE HILL MODEL, 18 Nov 2026 (Denis, 15 Nov: "use the same design
 * and structure for the next articles"; ops README, "The structure rule,
 * applied"). Camden was built before the model, so it was the one area page
 * without drawings or price cards. Kept as settled: the title and H1, the
 * kicker on main's wording, Lorenzo I's review, the two register photographs
 * and the labelled AI example (areas.ts), the address. Changed:
 *   - A line drawing beside each section that describes something
 *     (src/data/area-drawings.ts): a terrace with a continuous first-floor
 *     balcony, wardrobes either side of a dormer window, the front and back
 *     rooms from above with alcove units, the stair protected on a delivery
 *     day. None beside the listed homes section, which is rules and records.
 *   - The guide prices as cards (PriceCards.astro), with the lines approved
 *     on Hampstead; the service pages' own figures (verify checks them).
 *   - The opening carries "bespoke carpentry" and "bespoke joinery" once
 *     each and keeps the Camden Town base line. Kaspar's 6 Sep sentence on
 *     where we work came out (26 words, and the Hampstead draft carries it).
 *   - Three new FAQs (below).
 *
 * CHECKED 18 Nov by three independent reviewers (copy rules, every fact
 * against the facts file and his approved text, the swap test against the
 * nine other area pages). Changed on their findings: lines with no source
 * went ("many are now flats", "and flats" in the description, "built with a
 * chimney breast in most rooms", "the front wall leans in", the visitor
 * permit for the van, fixings around skirting and panelling); the sentences
 * other pages share gave way to Camden-only facts (Arlington Road's roof
 * form, Tudor Lodge, the Jeffreys Street house, Albert Street's front gardens,
 * Camden Square's steps) and the hanging depth, "two windows wide", the sash
 * line and Kelly Street are gone (Primrose Hill, Kentish Town and Islington
 * carry them); four headings now name Camden Town or are this page's own;
 * the appraisal is named in full once; cornice and dormer are explained; the
 * FAQ answers keep to his words and no longer repeat Hampstead's warranty
 * line.
 *
 * FAQ, all three from Google People also ask (UK, English, desktop, the
 * built-in browser, 18 Nov 2026; Reject all on consent). None repeats the page
 * and no other area page asks it.
 *   - "What are bespoke fitted wardrobes?" (fitted wardrobes camden; also in
 *     that box on 14 Nov). The first sentence is ours; the rest is q13
 *     (drawings where the design needs them), q20 (what they show) and q38
 *     (drawings kept on file).
 *   - "How long do fitted wardrobes last?" (fitted wardrobes camden). From
 *     q41 (chosen for long-term stability; how veneers and painted finishes
 *     age), q43 (solid timber rarely makes sense; engineered boards more
 *     stable), q27 ("strong and true over time") and q37 (care advice). No
 *     lifespan in years: none is approved, and the guarantee is already in
 *     the price section.
 *   - "How thick should alcove shelves be?" (alcove units). From the alcove
 *     units guide he approved ("Shelf construction depends on the span and
 *     intended contents"; "Material, thickness, reinforcement and fixings are
 *     considered together. Wider shelves may need concealed reinforcement or
 *     a supporting division"; "A thick appearance alone does not establish
 *     how much a shelf can carry"). No thickness in millimetres: none is
 *     approved.
 *   Dropped: "How much does it cost to fit fitted wardrobes?" (it restated the
 *   price section) and "Is 500mm deep enough for a wardrobe?" (it restated the
 *   hanging depth). Not used: the cost and brand questions in the same boxes,
 *   "Is MDF or plywood better for wardrobes?" (he took plywood out of the range
 *   on 6 Sep), "What is the 70/30 wardrobe rule?" (no fixed meaning) and the
 *   trend and depth questions. bespoke wardrobes camden and alcove units camden
 *   showed no box.
 *
 * 19 NOV 2026, DENIS'S COMMENTS ON THE REVIEW LINK.
 *   - The opening: "if for this article the old copy protocol was used update
 *     this section. If not, i dont see much value for the viewer here". It was
 *     the 14 Nov wording, written to protocol v1.4 before the newsroom playbook
 *     and the area page rules v1.0 (17 Nov) existed; the 18 Nov move kept it.
 *     Rewritten to the area rules' section 4 item 2 (the playbook's summary
 *     lede): the first sentence answers the search, with the approved Camden
 *     Town base folded in, and the council appraisal sentence went (the
 *     playbook's "unnecessary geography" in an opening). Facts: based in Camden
 *     Town (home page); early and mid 19th century terraces, the planned grid
 *     west of the High Street (S1; Mornington Crescent has no date there); "Walls, floors and
 *     ceilings are rarely straight" (q31, his words, kept general). Not Primrose
 *     Hill's "measured in the room and cut to its walls" construction (swap
 *     test). "bespoke carpentry" and "bespoke joinery" once each.
 *   - Checked the same day by four independent passes (copy rules with the
 *     playbook, facts, swap test, the render protocol). Changed on them: the
 *     appraisal is named where it is first used (Terraces), since the opening
 *     no longer names it; the railway cuttings and Carreras factory line went
 *     (no use to the reader) and "mansard" is explained; the alcove section
 *     leads with the alcove point; "historic doors and skirting in places"
 *     (S5's own limit); "engineered boards are more stable" (q43's word); the
 *     parking page "has a search by street or postcode" (S11). Left for a
 *     founder or Kaspar: the FAQ "How long do fitted wardrobes last?" opens on
 *     materials, not a lifespan (none is approved, and the guarantee would
 *     repeat the price section); the dormer window seat (his card); "we agree
 *     with you where the van stops" (as on Primrose Hill, no source in the
 *     facts file).
 *   - Pictures: no AI label (main's PRs #66 and #67, merged into the stack);
 *     the grey render lost a framed picture that ran into the door frame; ssp-029
 *     gave way to a new render (areas.ts).
 *
 * OPEN FOR A FOUNDER: the kicker. Main says "Bespoke joinery in Camden";
 * Arthur's comment branch (flowtoform/areas-comments-2026-11-14, never merged)
 * says "Bespoke carpentry in Camden". Main's wording stays until a founder
 * decides. Worth asking Kaspar on the page: whether he takes on a window seat
 * between wardrobes in a dormer (the guide's loft example shows one; Kentish
 * Town's card asks the same about bays).
 */
import AreaPage from "../../components/AreaPage.astro";
import Wrap from "../../components/ds/Wrap.astro";
import SectionHead from "../../components/ds/SectionHead.astro";
import Reviews from "../../components/ds/Reviews.astro";
import Faq from "../../components/ds/Faq.astro";
import Link from "../../components/ds/Link.astro";
import AreaSide from "../../components/AreaSide.astro";
import PriceCards from "../../components/PriceCards.astro";

import { AREAS } from "../../data/areas";
import { QUOTE_BANK, REVIEWS } from "../../data/site-content";
import { ANSWERS, answered } from "../../data/answers";

const area = AREAS.find((a) => a.slug === "areas/camden")!;

/* Every bank entry tagged with this district, whole: Lorenzo I's. */
const quotes = QUOTE_BANK.filter((q) => q.areas.includes(area.name)).map((q) => ({
  text: q.text,
  meta: q.gate && answered(q.gate) ? String(ANSWERS[q.gate].value) : q.meta,
}));

/* The lines approved on Hampstead, with the service pages' own figures; scripts/verify-build.mjs checks each one. */
const PRICES = [
  {
    art: "fitted-wardrobes",
    name: "Fitted wardrobes",
    href: "/fitted-wardrobes/",
    price: "From £2,300 for a 2-door run up to 1.2m in melamine, and from £4,000 at 4 doors.",
    moves: "Width, finish, internal drawers, integrated lighting and more complex designs move the figure.",
  },
  {
    art: "alcove-units",
    name: "Alcove units",
    href: "/alcove-units/",
    price: "From £3,300 for a pair in melamine, and from £6,000 in oak veneer.",
    moves: "Finish, shelving detail, drawers and lighting move the figure. It assumes base cupboards with shelving over, up to about 1m per alcove.",
  },
  {
    art: "tv-units",
    name: "Built-in TV units",
    href: "/media-walls/",
    price: "From £3,500.",
    moves: "Overall size, cabinetry and storage, integrated lighting, cable management and specialist finishes move the figure.",
  },
];
const P2 = "margin-top:var(--space-10)";
const TEXT = "max-width:var(--measure)";
const SECTION = "padding-bottom:var(--section-y)";
const EXT = { target: "_blank", rel: "noopener" };
---

<AreaPage area={area}>
  <Fragment slot="lede">
    <p>
      Based in Camden Town, we take on bespoke carpentry for the houses around us: <Link href="/fitted-wardrobes/">fitted wardrobes</Link>, <Link href="/alcove-units/">alcove units</Link> and shelving.
    </p>
    <p style={P2}>
      Many of those houses are early and mid 19th century terraces, like those on the planned grid west of the High Street. Walls, floors and ceilings are rarely straight, so each piece of bespoke joinery is fitted to the room as it is.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Terraces" level={4}>Fitted wardrobes in the terraces west of Camden High Street</SectionHead>
    <AreaSide drawing="balcony-terrace">
      <p>
        West of Camden High Street, Albert Street, Arlington Road and Mornington Terrace were laid out on a planned grid between 1820 and 1850. Camden Council's appraisal of the Camden Town Conservation Area describes yellow stock-brick terraces, typically three storeys over a basement. Most have painted timber sash windows, and cast-iron railings are a feature.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        Mornington Terrace, built in the 1840s, is taller and grander than the streets to its east, with continuous cast-iron balconies. Albert Street was built mostly by George Bassett between 1844 and 1848, and many of its houses are still single family homes.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        In a family house, note what ends up on a chair or in another room before the wardrobes are drawn. Those things show what the new layout needs to solve. A drawer section needs a door opening that gives access to its full width, so the inside and the doors are worked out together.
      </p>
    </AreaSide>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Mansard rooms" level={4}>Wardrobes under the mansards of Mornington Crescent and Albert Street</SectionHead>
    <AreaSide drawing="dormer-wardrobes">
      <p>
        Mornington Crescent is three curved terraces of four storeys over basements. Their mansard roofs have a steep lower slope, with dormers: windows built out from the slope of the roof. In Albert Street, Nos 22 to 46 are the only terrace that keeps its original mansard attic.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        In rooms like these, wardrobes can stand either side of a dormer, with a window seat between them.
      </p>
    </AreaSide>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Alcoves" level={4}>Alcove units in Camden Town's front and back rooms</SectionHead>
    <AreaSide drawing="front-back-alcoves">
      <p>
        Where a room has a chimney breast, an alcove unit fills the recess on either side. A floor with a breast in each room can take two pairs of units, each drawn for its own alcove.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        The houses on the west side of Arlington Road date from the 1820s to about 1840. The appraisal calls their roof form very distinct, with large rear chimney stacks and long stair windows.
      </p>
    </AreaSide>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Listed homes" level={4}>Checking whether a Camden Town house is listed</SectionHead>
    <div style={TEXT}>
      <p>
        Jeffreys Street's north terrace, built around 1816 to 1825, is listed at Grade II. Tudor Lodge in Albert Street, of 1843, is listed too: the appraisal calls it reputedly London's earliest surviving purpose-built artist's studio. Camden's <Link href="https://www.camden.gov.uk/listed-buildings" {...EXT}>listed buildings page</Link> has a map that shows whether your house is on the list.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        Camden's protection of a listed house covers its interior, so significant work inside can need listed building consent.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        A heritage report on Camden's planning file records one Jeffreys Street house's original staircase and plaster cornice, with historic doors and skirting in places. Where a room keeps details like these, a wardrobe can finish neatly below the cornice, the moulding where the wall meets the ceiling.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Access" level={4}>Parking and deliveries in Camden Town</SectionHead>
    <AreaSide drawing="stair-protection">
      <p>
        South of Delancey Street, Albert Street's houses sit behind front gardens up to 5 metres deep. In Camden Square, steep steps climb to front doors above semi-basements. Before installation we agree with you where the van stops and how each piece comes in.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        Camden sells <Link href="https://www.camden.gov.uk/trade-permits" {...EXT}>trade permits</Link> to tradespeople working for a resident, valid in resident bays and on certain yellow lines, with no space promised. Its <Link href="https://www.camden.gov.uk/controlled-parking-zones" {...EXT}>parking zone page</Link> has a search by street or postcode.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        Where the stairs are tight, large pieces come in as sections ready to assemble. Stair protection goes down on every day material comes in.
      </p>
    </AreaSide>
  </Wrap>

  {/* "From N Google reviews" and the link to all of them are the component's. */}
  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <Reviews
      title=""
      average={REVIEWS.average}
      count={REVIEWS.count}
      href={REVIEWS.profileUrl}
      quotes={quotes}
    />
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Guide prices" level={4}>Guide prices for Camden Town wardrobes and alcove units</SectionHead>
    <AreaSide>
      <p>Every figure here is a starting price, and a larger or more detailed piece costs more.</p>
      <p style={P2}>
        The measured survey comes first, then a fixed, itemised quotation. Our <Link href="/fitted-wardrobe-cost/">cost guide</Link> lists the starting price for each wardrobe size and finish.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        The guarantee covers installation and workmanship for 2 years and the structure of the joinery for 10 years. If a door or drawer needs adjusting in the first 2 years, we come back and adjust it.
      </p>
      <PriceCards slot="side" items={PRICES} />
    </AreaSide>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Questions" scribe>Asked before enquiring</SectionHead>
    <div style="margin-top:var(--space-12);max-width:var(--measure);border-bottom:var(--rule-hair)">
      <Faq q="What are bespoke fitted wardrobes?">
        They are wardrobes designed for one room rather than bought in standard sizes. Where a design needs drawings, they show the inside layout and the materials and finishes before work starts. The drawings stay on file, so a matching piece later starts from that record.
      </Faq>
      <Faq q="How long do fitted wardrobes last?">
        The materials are chosen for long-term stability: engineered boards are more stable than solid timber, which rarely makes sense for fitted furniture. Natural veneers develop subtly with age and light, while painted and decorative finishes stay more consistent. Care advice for each finish comes with the finished piece.
      </Faq>
      <Faq q="How thick should alcove shelves be?">
        Shelf thickness depends on how far the shelf spans between supports and what it holds: books ask more of it than a light display. Material, thickness, reinforcement and fixings are considered together, and a wider shelf may need concealed reinforcement or a supporting division. A thick appearance alone does not show how much a shelf can carry.
      </Faq>
    </div>
  </Wrap>
</AreaPage>
